e bit  noisy or if i'm sitting in an airplane  then i use these  which are made by a company called  sennheiser and they have a noise guard  thing so you can actually block out  noise but i don't use these very much  because of course my ears will start to  sweat  uh you know when i'm working out so but  for 15-20 minutes on the nordic track  they're fine so invest in quality sound  most of the mp3 players will produce  good sound make sure you have decent uh  earphones other than that here quickly  as i'm running out i want this to be  short basic principles of listening  you're going to tune out don't worry  about it  when you start new in a language at  least i find that i want to listen 20 30  times and then as i progress i listen  less and less frequently to the same  content so if i basically  kind of stop focusing go away from the  listening it doesn't matter listen again  you'll eventually pick up all the  different spots  uh try to  for most of the period until i'm very  good i always want to have a text of  what i'm listening to so that i can then  read it and save words and phrases or  underline phrases focus on the text so  that when i listen again i start to  notice these things i'm not one of those  but there are people who like to do this  i'm not one of those who just likes to  have  the  language sort of  blaring on in the background i like to  actually try to understand what i'm  listening to  and i like to focus on different words  and phrases even if i'm not focusing all  the time  uh listening is is just a big advantage  is you can do it anywhere you want it  enables you to basically  fill in dead time if i had to rely on  other activities i wouldn't study nearly  as much 80 of my language learning  activity is based on listening and if  you listen the way i advise you to you  will find that certain phrases are going  to stay with you phrases that you  listened to two years ago are still  going to be with you  today kind of thing so those are my tips  don't worry if you can't focus all the  time  i find it difficult to focus if i  actually sit down and try to listen but  whereas if i'm doing something else i  find that i am able to focus 60 70 80 of  the time and that really helps the brain  get used to the language so  that's my take on how to listen i hope  that's useful
 

